Title:
naively bypassing new memory scanning POCs

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post analyzes recent proof-of-concept “memory scanning” approaches and shows straightforward ways to bypass them.  
- It focuses on why simplistic scanners (e.g., scanning for known byte patterns, RWX pages, or obvious shellcode traits) are brittle and produce easy evasion opportunities.  
- The author discusses practical evasion ideas such as payload transformation/encoding, staging/decryption at runtime, avoiding suspicious memory protections, and reducing recognizable artifacts in-process.  
- It’s primarily useful for red teamers, exploit developers, and malware developers evaluating how resilient their in-memory tradecraft is against emerging “scan memory” tooling.  
- It’s interesting because it highlights the gap between PoC memory scanners and real-world detection, emphasizing attacker adaptability and the need for behavioral/telemetry-driven defenses.

Technical Focus:
- In-memory payload detection limitations
- Shellcode artifact reduction and transformation
- Memory protection strategies (RW/RX vs RWX)
- Runtime decryption/staging and delayed materialization
- Signature/pattern-based scanning evasion

Use Cases:
- Evaluating whether a payload is trivially detectable by basic memory scanners
- Hardening in-memory loaders/stagers against naive signature scans
- Designing more realistic defensive memory scanning heuristics
- Building test cases to benchmark EDR-like memory inspection approaches

Keywords:
memory scanning, shellcode, in-memory detection, signature-based detection, YARA-like patterns, RWX, RX, VirtualProtect, staged payload, runtime decryption, artifact reduction, evasion, loader, stager, process injection, heuristic detection, PoC limitations